  "hints": [
    "Which open bracket must the next closing bracket match: the first one you saw, or the most recent unmatched one?",
    "Push each opening bracket on a stack. A closing bracket must match the bracket on top.",
    "Fail when a closer arrives with an empty stack or the wrong kind on top. At the end, the stack must be empty."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function isBalanced(text) {\n  const opener = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' }\n  const stack = []\n  for (const ch of text) {\n    if (ch in opener) {\n      if (stack.pop() !== opener[ch]) return false\n    } else {\n      stack.push(ch)\n    }\n  }\n  return stack.length === 0\n}\n",
    "py": "def is_balanced(text):\n    opener = {\")\": \"(\", \"]\": \"[\", \"}\": \"{\"}\n    stack = []\n    for ch in text:\n        if ch in opener:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != opener[ch]:\n                return False\n        else:\n            stack.append(ch)\n    return not stack\n",
    "explanation": "The most recently opened bracket must be closed first, which is exactly last-in, first-out order. Each closer pops the stack and must match the popped opener; popping an empty stack (undefined in JS) or leaving openers behind means the line is unbalanced."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "notes": "With only one bracket kind a counter would do; several kinds need the stack to remember the order of openers."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Return the index of the first character that makes the line unbalanced (or the length if openers are left over).",
    "How would you skip brackets inside quoted strings?"
  ]
